#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562597 :question/problem "Given that $u(r,\\theta)=3 r^{3} (\\cos {4 \\theta} - 2)$, find $\\frac{∂u}{∂x}$, where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂x}=\\frac{∂u}{∂r}\\cos \\theta-\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂u}{∂\\theta}\\sin \\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $u$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂r}=9 r^{2} (\\cos {4 \\theta} - 2),\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂\\theta}=- 12 r^{3} \\sin {4 \\theta}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =(9 r^{2} (\\cos {4 \\theta} - 2))\\cos \\theta-\\frac{1}{r}(- 12 r^{3} \\sin {4 \\theta})\\sin \\theta \\\\ & =-18{r}^{2}\\cos \\theta+9{r}^{2}\\cos 4\\theta\\cos \\theta+12{r}^{2}\\sin 4\\theta\\sin \\theta \\\\ & =3{r}^{2}(-6\\cos \\theta+3\\cos 4\\theta\\cos \\theta+4\\sin 4\\theta\\sin \\theta).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562601 :question/problem "Given that $h(r,\\theta)=- 4 r^{3}$, find $\\frac{∂h}{∂x}$, where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂h}{∂x}=\\frac{∂h}{∂r}\\cos \\theta-\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂h}{∂\\theta}\\sin \\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $h$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂r}=- 12 r^{2},\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂\\theta}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =(- 12 r^{2})\\cos \\theta-\\frac{1}{r}(0)\\sin \\theta \\\\ & =- 12 r^{2} \\cos {\\theta}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562614 :question/problem "Given that $g(r,\\theta)=3 r^{3}$, find $\\frac{∂g}{∂x}$, where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂g}{∂x}=\\frac{∂g}{∂r}\\cos \\theta-\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂g}{∂\\theta}\\sin \\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $g$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂r}=9 r^{2},\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂\\theta}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =(9 r^{2})\\cos \\theta-\\frac{1}{r}(0)\\sin \\theta \\\\ & =9 r^{2} \\cos {\\theta}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562628 :question/problem "Given that $w(r,\\theta)=- 2 r^{2}$, find $\\frac{∂w}{∂y}$, where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂w}{∂y}=\\frac{∂w}{∂r}\\sin \\theta+\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta}\\cos \\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂r}=- 4 r,\\,\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =(- 4 r)\\sin \\theta+\\frac{1}{r}(0)\\cos \\theta \\\\ & =- 4 r \\sin {\\theta}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562629 :question/problem "Given that $w(r,\\theta)=- 2 r^{3} (\\cos {3 \\theta} - 3)$, find $\\frac{∂w}{∂y}$, where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂w}{∂y}=\\frac{∂w}{∂r}\\sin \\theta+\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta}\\cos \\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂r}=- 6 r^{2} (\\cos {3 \\theta} - 3),\\,\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta}=6 r^{3} \\sin {3 \\theta}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =(- 6 r^{2} (\\cos {3 \\theta} - 3))\\sin \\theta+\\frac{1}{r}(6 r^{3} \\sin {3 \\theta})\\cos \\theta \\\\ & =18{r}^{2}\\sin \\theta-6{r}^{2}\\cos 3\\theta\\sin \\theta+6{r}^{2}\\sin 3\\theta\\cos \\theta \\\\ & =-2{r}^{2}(-9\\sin \\theta+3\\cos 3\\theta\\sin \\theta-3\\sin 3\\theta\\cos \\theta).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562634 :question/problem "Given that $f(r,\\theta)=r^{2}$, find $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$, where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂r}\\cos \\theta-\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta}\\sin \\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂r}=2 r,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =(2 r)\\cos \\theta-\\frac{1}{r}(0)\\sin \\theta \\\\ & =2 r \\cos {\\theta}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562637 :question/problem "Given that $u(r,\\theta)=- 3 r^{3} (\\sin {2 \\theta} - 3)$, find $\\frac{∂u}{∂x}$, where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂x}=\\frac{∂u}{∂r}\\cos \\theta-\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂u}{∂\\theta}\\sin \\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $u$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂r}=- 9 r^{2} (\\sin {2 \\theta} - 3),\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂\\theta}=- 6 r^{3} \\cos {2 \\theta}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =(- 9 r^{2} (\\sin {2 \\theta} - 3))\\cos \\theta-\\frac{1}{r}(- 6 r^{3} \\cos {2 \\theta})\\sin \\theta \\\\ & =27{r}^{2}\\cos \\theta-9{r}^{2}\\sin 2\\theta\\cos \\theta+6{r}^{2}\\cos 2\\theta\\sin \\theta \\\\ & =-3{r}^{2}(-9\\cos \\theta+3\\sin 2\\theta\\cos \\theta-2\\cos 2\\theta\\sin \\theta).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562653 :question/problem "Given that $u(r,\\theta)=- 4 r^{2}$, find $\\frac{∂u}{∂y}$, where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂y}=\\frac{∂u}{∂r}\\sin \\theta+\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂u}{∂\\theta}\\cos \\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $u$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂r}=- 8 r,\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂\\theta}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =(- 8 r)\\sin \\theta+\\frac{1}{r}(0)\\cos \\theta \\\\ & =- 8 r \\sin {\\theta}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562656 :question/problem "Given that $h(r,\\theta)=- 3 r^{2} (\\cos {4 \\theta} - 1)$, find $\\frac{∂h}{∂x}$, where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂h}{∂x}=\\frac{∂h}{∂r}\\cos \\theta-\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂h}{∂\\theta}\\sin \\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $h$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂r}=- 6 r (\\cos {4 \\theta} - 1),\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂\\theta}=12 r^{2} \\sin {4 \\theta}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =(- 6 r (\\cos {4 \\theta} - 1))\\cos \\theta-\\frac{1}{r}(12 r^{2} \\sin {4 \\theta})\\sin \\theta \\\\ & =6r\\cos \\theta-6r\\cos 4\\theta\\cos \\theta-12r\\sin 4\\theta\\sin \\theta \\\\ & =-3r(-2\\cos \\theta+2\\cos 4\\theta\\cos \\theta+4\\sin 4\\theta\\sin \\theta).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562768 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-4x^2y)\\,dx+(y+4xy^2)\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is the counterclockwise boundary of the disk with polar description $0\\le r\\le 2,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/d2/d2ee5fe463ad086cce741c62866c38c7ea331b343bdb0ae82ab64a0f8e730a1e.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=e^{-x^2}-4x^2y,\\quad Q(x,y)=y+4xy^2,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=- 4 x^{2},\\quad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=4 y^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-4x^2y)\\,dx+(y+4xy^2)\\,dy&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA \\\\ &=\\iint_D\\left(4 y^{2}-(- 4 x^{2})\\right)\\,dA \\\\ &=\\iint_D 4(x^2+y^2)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 2,0\\le\\theta\\le 2 \\pi\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 4(x^2+y^2)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta 4(r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta))\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=4\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{2}r^3(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=4\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{2}r^3\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=4\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left[\\frac{r^4}{4}\\right]_{0}^{2}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=4\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left(4-0\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=4\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}4\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=16[\\theta]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ &=16\\left(2 \\pi-0\\right) \\\\ &=32 \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562773 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-x^2y)\\,dx+(y+xy^2)\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is the counterclockwise boundary of the disk with polar description $0\\le r\\le 1,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/ee/eed043488a6cd3949da95907125dcdfa2f946feefff60772495e48e9610c4bc1.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=e^{-x^2}-x^2y,\\quad Q(x,y)=y+xy^2,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=- x^{2},\\quad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=y^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-x^2y)\\,dx+(y+xy^2)\\,dy&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA \\\\ &=\\iint_D\\left(y^{2}-(- x^{2})\\right)\\,dA \\\\ &=\\iint_D (x^2+y^2)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 1,0\\le\\theta\\le 2 \\pi\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D (x^2+y^2)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta (r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta))\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{1}r^3(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{1}r^3\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left[\\frac{r^4}{4}\\right]_{0}^{1}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left(\\frac{1}{4}-0\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\frac{1}{4}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{4}[\\theta]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{4}\\left(2 \\pi-0\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{\\pi}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562783 :question/problem "![](images/5d/5def443330d0f3793a305101ad63ea19d8a6e0a7835a23018063bd6fb57d23a8.png)\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C(\\sqrt{5+x}-5x^2y)\\,dx+(e^{-y^2}+5xy^2)\\,dy,\n$$ where the path $C$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/3b/3ba969ce97031cecfc6c60ce908a082b268c11a474e6a588b9719ef2ce39e0d2.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\sqrt{5+x}-5x^2y,\\quad Q(x,y)=e^{-y^2}+5xy^2,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=- 5 x^{2},\\quad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=5 y^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(\\sqrt{5+x}-5x^2y)\\,dx+(e^{-y^2}+5xy^2)\\,dy&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA \\\\ &=\\iint_D\\left(5 y^{2}-(- 5 x^{2})\\right)\\,dA \\\\ &=\\iint_D 5(x^2+y^2)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):1\\le r\\le 2,0\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 5(x^2+y^2)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta 5(r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta))\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=5\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{1}^{2}r^3(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=5\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{1}^{2}r^3\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=5\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left[\\frac{r^4}{4}\\right]_{1}^{2}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=5\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(4-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=5\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\frac{15}{4}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\frac{75}{4}[\\theta]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\ &=\\frac{75}{4}\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}-0\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{75 \\pi}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562793 :question/problem "![](images/9f/9f98c6d615262205474643c73e3d4b5bd8d94a8560d0ffbda34ad7aca362d1bd.png)\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C(\\sqrt{x+1}-x^2y)\\,dx+(e^y+xy^2)\\,dy,\n$$ where the path $C$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/50/50aabd9d34ec0f889ca832e181e271e3e5918f936708684a125fadcdb1cdb71f.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\sqrt{x+1}-x^2y,\\quad Q(x,y)=e^y+xy^2,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=- x^{2},\\quad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=y^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(\\sqrt{x+1}-x^2y)\\,dx+(e^y+xy^2)\\,dy&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA \\\\ &=\\iint_D\\left(y^{2}-(- x^{2})\\right)\\,dA \\\\ &=\\iint_D (x^2+y^2)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 4,0\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D (x^2+y^2)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta (r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta))\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{4}r^3(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{4}r^3\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left[\\frac{r^4}{4}\\right]_{0}^{4}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(64-0\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}64\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=64[\\theta]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\ &=64\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}-0\\right) \\\\ &=32 \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562801 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-x^2y)\\,dx+(y+xy^2)\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is the counterclockwise boundary of the disk with polar description $0\\le r\\le 3,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/6f/6f7b5381fa7792a722e515878be7e2a964d85b9e7f5a17aa83b3983b7f2a7bf5.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=e^{-x^2}-x^2y,\\quad Q(x,y)=y+xy^2,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=- x^{2},\\quad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=y^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-x^2y)\\,dx+(y+xy^2)\\,dy&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA \\\\ &=\\iint_D\\left(y^{2}-(- x^{2})\\right)\\,dA \\\\ &=\\iint_D (x^2+y^2)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 3,0\\le\\theta\\le 2 \\pi\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D (x^2+y^2)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta (r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta))\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{3}r^3(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{3}r^3\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left[\\frac{r^4}{4}\\right]_{0}^{3}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left(\\frac{81}{4}-0\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\frac{81}{4}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\frac{81}{4}[\\theta]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ &=\\frac{81}{4}\\left(2 \\pi-0\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{81 \\pi}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562805 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-3x^2y)\\,dx+(y+3xy^2)\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is the counterclockwise boundary of the disk with polar description $0\\le r\\le 3,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/6f/6f7b5381fa7792a722e515878be7e2a964d85b9e7f5a17aa83b3983b7f2a7bf5.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=e^{-x^2}-3x^2y,\\quad Q(x,y)=y+3xy^2,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=- 3 x^{2},\\quad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=3 y^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-3x^2y)\\,dx+(y+3xy^2)\\,dy&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA \\\\ &=\\iint_D\\left(3 y^{2}-(- 3 x^{2})\\right)\\,dA \\\\ &=\\iint_D 3(x^2+y^2)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 3,0\\le\\theta\\le 2 \\pi\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 3(x^2+y^2)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta 3(r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta))\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=3\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{3}r^3(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=3\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{3}r^3\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=3\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left[\\frac{r^4}{4}\\right]_{0}^{3}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=3\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left(\\frac{81}{4}-0\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=3\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\frac{81}{4}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\frac{243}{4}[\\theta]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ &=\\frac{243}{4}\\left(2 \\pi-0\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{243 \\pi}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562808 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-3x^2y)\\,dx+(y+3xy^2)\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is the counterclockwise boundary of the disk with polar description $0\\le r\\le 4,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/f0/f070b659b344abe432eb2218cddc02947cecca2e7dafb89a6bf3a49dc4ba06af.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=e^{-x^2}-3x^2y,\\quad Q(x,y)=y+3xy^2,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=- 3 x^{2},\\quad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=3 y^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-3x^2y)\\,dx+(y+3xy^2)\\,dy&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA \\\\ &=\\iint_D\\left(3 y^{2}-(- 3 x^{2})\\right)\\,dA \\\\ &=\\iint_D 3(x^2+y^2)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 4,0\\le\\theta\\le 2 \\pi\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 3(x^2+y^2)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta 3(r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta))\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=3\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{4}r^3(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=3\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{4}r^3\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=3\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left[\\frac{r^4}{4}\\right]_{0}^{4}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=3\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left(64-0\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=3\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}64\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=192[\\theta]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ &=192\\left(2 \\pi-0\\right) \\\\ &=384 \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563650 :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)={y}^{x}.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,e)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf{v}=⟨4,-3⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,e)$ in the direction of $v$ is equal to the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,e).$ \n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-3)^{2}}=5 \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨4,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,e):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{x}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{x})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{y}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,x{y}^{x-1}⟩ \\\\ ∇g(1,e) & =⟨{e}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡e,1{e}^{1-1}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨e,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,e)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(1,e) & =∇g(1,e)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨e,1⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}⟨4,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{e(4)+1(-3)}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{4e-3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563656 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=0.$ Given that the point $P(\\frac{1}{2},0,1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=(y+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=0.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(\\frac{1}{2},0).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(\\frac{1}{2},0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(\\frac{1}{2},0) & =(0+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2})+2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(\\frac{1}{2},0) & ={f}_{x}(\\frac{1}{2},0)(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ z-1 & =1(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ z-1 & =x-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ z & =x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(\\frac{1}{2},0,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=0 \\\\ z &=x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=x+\\frac{1}{2}$ (or $x-z+\\frac{1}{2}=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-i-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $-i-k$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-(-i-k)=i+k$ is also parallel to it."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563687 :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=2.$ Given that the point $P$ with $y$-coordinate $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy-\\frac{\\pi }{3})$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point where $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563689 :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},4)$ in the direction in which the function increases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,\\,\\frac{2}{y}⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,g(\\frac{\\pi }{4},4) & =⟨-\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right),\\,\\frac{2}{4}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},4)$ in the direction of $∇g(\\frac{\\pi }{4},4)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖∇\\,g(\\frac{\\pi }{4},4)‖ & =‖⟨-1,\\frac{1}{2}⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563713 :question/problem "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xyz$ increase most rapidly at the point $(1,0,-2)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,0,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xyz),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xyz),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xyz)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2x-yz,\\,2y-xz,\\,2z-xy⟩ \\\\ ∇g(1,0,-2) & =⟨2(1)-0(-2),2(0)-1(-2),2(-2)-1(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,2,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨2,2,-4⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563725 :question/problem "The coefficients $a$ and $b$ also satisfy the equation $100a+15b=4\\,800.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a\\,=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$b\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}b &=270-15a \\\\ 100a+15b &=4\\,800\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nNotice that the first equation is already solved for $b:$\n\n$$\nb=270-15a\n$$\n\nSo, we replace $b$ in the second equation and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100a+15b & =4\\,800 \\\\ 100a+15(270-15a) & =4\\,800 \\\\ 100a+4\\,050-225a & =4\\,800 \\\\ -125a+4\\,050 & =4\\,800 \\\\ -125a & =750 \\\\ a & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $a=-6$ into $b=270-15a,$ we can then find the value of $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =270-15(-6) \\\\ b & =360\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=-6,\\,b=360.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563730 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Given that the point $P(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})} & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})-1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})}(x-1) \\\\ z-0 & =2(x-1) \\\\ z & =2x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ z &=2x-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563741 :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=(2{x}^{2}-y)^{2}$ at the point $A(-1,1)$ in the direction towards the point $B(-2,2).$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨-2,2⟩-⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}}⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-y)^{2},\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-y)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨16{x}^{3}-8xy,\\,2y-4{x}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ∇f(-1,1) & =⟨16(-1)^{3}-8(-1)(1),\\,2(1)-4(-1)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-8,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(-1,1) & =∇f(-1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨-8,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =(-8)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)-2\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563750 :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-6xy+{y}^{2}$ at the point $(-1,3)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}.$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(-1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-6xy+{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-6xy+{y}^{2})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(2x-6y)\\,i+(2y-6x)j \\\\ ∇\\,f(-1,3) & =(2(-1)-6(3))\\,i+(2(3)-6(-1))j \\\\  & =-20\\,i+12\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(-1,3) & =∇\\,f(-1,3)\\cdot u \\\\  & =(-20\\,i+12\\,j)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{-20(1)+12(1)}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =-4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563760 :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf{v}=2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}.$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{5} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}(2i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+\\frac{y}{x})\\,i+(\\frac{x}{y}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\,j \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,1) & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+\\frac{1}{1})\\,i+(\\frac{1}{1}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\,j \\\\  & =i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(1,1) & =∇\\,f(1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =(i+j)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563763 :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{tan}⁡y+2{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{tan}⁡y+2{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{tan}⁡y+2{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{tan}⁡y+4x-\\frac{1}{x})i-x{sec}^{2}⁡y\\text{ }j \\\\ -∇f(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =-(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+4\\cdot 1-\\frac{1}{1})i-1\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-4\\,i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $-4\\,i-2\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563824 :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_C F\\cdot dr=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^{6}\\boxed{}\\,dr\\,d\\theta,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=⟨3{y}^{3}+{e}^{x},\\,\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-6{x}^{3}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle $x^2+y^2=36$ oriented counterclockwise, what expression belongs in the blank after applying Green’s theorem and changing to polar coordinates, simplified pointwise before integration?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(3{y}^{3}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+(\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-6{x}^{3})\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/47/47ee81cb3603c75c8a83dca2df1153733902576892fa6eed3df4bccd329655ee.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=3{y}^{3}+{e}^{x}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-6{x}^{3},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=9{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-18{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(3{y}^{3}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+(\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-6{x}^{3})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\ & ={∬}_{D}(-18{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 6,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,drd\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(-18{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(-18\\cdot (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-9\\cdot (r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={∬}_{Δ}(-18{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -9{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={∬}_{Δ}(-18{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -9{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}-18{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -9{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563828 :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_C F\\cdot dr=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^{5}\\boxed{}\\,dr\\,d\\theta,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=⟨3{y}^{3}+{e}^{x},\\,\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-2{x}^{3}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle $x^2+y^2=25$ oriented counterclockwise, what expression belongs in the blank after applying Green’s theorem and changing to polar coordinates, simplified pointwise before integration?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(3{y}^{3}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+(\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-2{x}^{3})\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/ee/ee7096a5ce1f420ac962f93b0efb47a00cdc2c9d9ec69f66b6340d02dd106b77.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=3{y}^{3}+{e}^{x}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-2{x}^{3},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=9{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-6{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(3{y}^{3}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+(\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-2{x}^{3})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\ & ={∬}_{D}(-6{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,drd\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(-6{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(-6\\cdot (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-9\\cdot (r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={∬}_{Δ}(-6{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -9{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={∬}_{Δ}(-6{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -9{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{5}-6{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -9{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563837 :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_C F\\cdot dr=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^{1}\\boxed{}\\,dr\\,d\\theta,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=⟨6{y}^{3}+{e}^{x},\\,\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-3{x}^{3}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle $x^2+y^2=1$ oriented counterclockwise, what expression belongs in the blank after applying Green’s theorem and changing to polar coordinates, simplified pointwise before integration?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(6{y}^{3}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+(\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-3{x}^{3})\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/fb/fbf107e5d55e2dd51307b821d9d2f5134810317c69706a57f8d9aac528f78e93.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=6{y}^{3}+{e}^{x}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-3{x}^{3},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=18{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-9{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(6{y}^{3}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+(\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-3{x}^{3})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\ & ={∬}_{D}(-9{x}^{2}-18{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,drd\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(-9{x}^{2}-18{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(-9\\cdot (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-18\\cdot (r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={∬}_{Δ}(-9{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -18{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={∬}_{Δ}(-9{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -18{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}-9{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -18{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563840 :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_C F\\cdot dr=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^{2}\\boxed{}\\,dr\\,d\\theta,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=⟨4{y}^{3}+{e}^{x},\\,\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-3{x}^{3}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle $x^2+y^2=4$ oriented counterclockwise, what expression belongs in the blank after applying Green’s theorem and changing to polar coordinates, simplified pointwise before integration?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(4{y}^{3}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+(\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-3{x}^{3})\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/06/06ecd3e3f2b2538556ffb2ffece12564b621f997ef8bf6de0e3297c7b86b393b.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=4{y}^{3}+{e}^{x}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-3{x}^{3},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=12{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-9{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(4{y}^{3}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+(\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-3{x}^{3})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\ & ={∬}_{D}(-9{x}^{2}-12{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,drd\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(-9{x}^{2}-12{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(-9\\cdot (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-12\\cdot (r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={∬}_{Δ}(-9{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -12{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={∬}_{Δ}(-9{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -12{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}-9{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -12{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563841 :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_C F\\cdot dr=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^{5}\\boxed{}\\,dr\\,d\\theta,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=⟨6{y}^{3}+{e}^{x},\\,\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-{x}^{3}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle $x^2+y^2=25$ oriented counterclockwise, what expression belongs in the blank after applying Green’s theorem and changing to polar coordinates, simplified pointwise before integration?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(6{y}^{3}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+(\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-{x}^{3})\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/ee/ee7096a5ce1f420ac962f93b0efb47a00cdc2c9d9ec69f66b6340d02dd106b77.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=6{y}^{3}+{e}^{x}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-{x}^{3},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=18{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(6{y}^{3}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+(\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-{x}^{3})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\ & ={∬}_{D}(-3{x}^{2}-18{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,drd\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(-3{x}^{2}-18{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(-3\\cdot (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-18\\cdot (r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={∬}_{Δ}(-3{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -18{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={∬}_{Δ}(-3{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -18{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{5}-3{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -18{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563844 :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $F(x,y)=⟨6y(x-1),\\,6x(y-1)⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation? Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, and simplify the integrand pointwise before integration." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}y(x-1)\\,dx+x(y-1)\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/fb/fbf107e5d55e2dd51307b821d9d2f5134810317c69706a57f8d9aac528f78e93.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=6y(x-1)$ and $Q(x,y)=6x(y-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=6(x-1),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=6(y-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y(x-1)\\,dx+x(y-1)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}((6(y-1))-(6(x-1)))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(6(y-x))\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(6(y-x))\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(6(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ))\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}6{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563860 :question/problem "Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle ∮_(C)F \\cdot dr = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3}\\boxed{}drdθ,\n$$\nwhere $F(x, y) = ⟨5xy^{2}, 5x + e^{\\cos y}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle $x^{2} + y^{2} = 9$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation? Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, and simplify the integrand pointwise before integration." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C F\\cdot dr=\\oint_C(5 x y^{2})\\,dx+(5 x + e^{\\cos{(y )}})\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/3b/3b1a8cf50ccf8ab3a73f31753def3c2fee2143a7372309cccf2ff7a5e68aedd9.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=5 x y^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=5 x + e^{\\cos{(y )}}$, we obtain $\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=10 x y$, $\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=5$.\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C F\\cdot dr=\\iint_D(5-(10 x y))\\,dA=\\iint_D(- 10 x y + 5)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as $\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 3,0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi\\}$.\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_D(- 10 x y + 5)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta(- 10 r^{2} \\sin{(\\theta )} \\cos{(\\theta )} + 5)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^{3}r (- 5 r^{2} \\sin{(2 \\theta )} + 5)\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563867 :question/problem "What is the amplitude of the function $f(x)=\\frac{8-10\\cos(x)}{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite the function as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{8-10\\cos(x)}{3}=-\\frac{10}{3}\\cdot \\cos(x)+\\frac{8}{3}\n$$\n\nNow, given a function in the form $f(x)=A\\cos(Bx+C)+D,$ the amplitude is $|A|.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $A=-\\frac{10}{3}.$ Therefore, the amplitude is $|A|=|-\\frac{10}{3}|=\\frac{10}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563874 :question/problem "What is the amplitude of the function $f(x)=8\\cos(4x+\\pi)?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $f(x)=A\\cos(Bx+C)+D,$ the amplitude is $|A|.$\n\nSince $A=8,$ we have that the amplitude is $|A|=|8|=8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563879 :question/problem "What is the amplitude of the function $f(x)=\\frac{4-2\\cos(x)}{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite the function as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{4-2\\cos(x)}{3}=-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\cos(x)+\\frac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nNow, given a function in the form $f(x)=A\\cos(Bx+C)+D,$ the amplitude is $|A|.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $A=-\\frac{2}{3}.$ Therefore, the amplitude is $|A|=|-\\frac{2}{3}|=\\frac{2}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563888 :question/problem "What is the amplitude of the function $f(x)=\\frac{10}{3}\\sin(6x+3)?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $f(x)=A\\sin(Bx+C)+D,$ the amplitude is $|A|.$\n\nSince $A=\\frac{10}{3},$ we have that the amplitude is $|A|=|\\frac{10}{3}|=\\frac{10}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563893 :question/problem "What is the amplitude of the function $f(x)=-4\\sin(6x+\\frac{1}{2})+6?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $f(x)=A\\sin(Bx+C)+D,$ the amplitude is $|A|.$\n\nSince $A=-4,$ we have that the amplitude is $|A|=|-4|=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563903 :question/problem "What is the amplitude of the function $f(x)=\\frac{9}{2}\\cos(5x+2)?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $f(x)=A\\cos(Bx+C)+D,$ the amplitude is $|A|.$\n\nSince $A=\\frac{9}{2},$ we have that the amplitude is $|A|=|\\frac{9}{2}|=\\frac{9}{2}.$"}]}
